So if you have fully autonomous&nbsp;
defense systems that can retaliate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:37.120
against what is perceived as an attack then&nbsp;
you could imagine this happening even without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:41.680
a real attack. On several occasions in&nbsp;
the history of nuclear weapons for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:49.040
our early detection systems have detected a&nbsp;
nuclear attack that wasn't actually happening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:53.680
It was only human intervention that prevented&nbsp;
a full-scale nuclear war from taking place.

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:05.120
The reason that we chose to make if human: kill()&nbsp;
was to pick up where Slaughterbots one left off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:11.200
Slaughterbots one warned us of an emerging risk&nbsp;
that we were going to face. When we presented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:19.600
Slaughterbots at the CCW in 2017, many people&nbsp;
dismissed it as science fiction. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:24.800
the Russian ambassador said, "why are we even&nbsp;
discussing this? This is 25 or 30 years in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:31.760
future. It's just science fiction." But when&nbsp;
we presented it to people in the AI community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:39.680
as they were watching the CEO demonstrating the&nbsp;
weapon and talking about its use for mass attacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:45.760
the AI researchers thought that this was a real&nbsp;
video, not a fictional presentation. But, this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:52.640
a real recording of a real CEO. So they obviously&nbsp;
considered the technology to be entirely feasible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.760 --> 00:21:00.640
Meanwhile, a Turkish company called STM was&nbsp;
preparing the public announcement for their weapon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:06.320
the Kargu autonomous dronel, which was announced&nbsp;
about three weeks after we presented the video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.360 --> 00:21:13.920
and advertised as having the capability for&nbsp;
autonomous hits using face recognition and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:20.160
human tracking. Kargu drones from the Turkish STM&nbsp;
company were used in Libya to hunt down fleeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:25.520
humans according to a United Nations report and&nbsp;
we just don't know whether these were soldiers or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:31.040
whether they were some random Libyan truck drivers&nbsp;
that had been contracted or what. And then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:36.080
company obviously is not sharing any information&nbsp;
with us. At the time, a lot of people were like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:40.320
"ah forget it they're decades and&nbsp;
decades away!" Well, they were wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:45.360
because now they're already here. And&nbsp;
so the next part of the story for us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:51.760
was to talk about what is the kind of thinking&nbsp;
that is fueling our development of these weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:55.920
and moving towards that horrific endpoint&nbsp;
that was shown in Slaughterbots one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.920 --> 00:22:01.280
but more importantly what is it that we can do&nbsp;
about it to avert that outcome? And how can we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:08.000
really take this opportunity of autonomous weapons&nbsp;
not only to avert a really dire outcome for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:15.280
humanity but also help steer AI and set a powerful&nbsp;
precedent for how AI can be used for good and be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:22.000
used safely and ethically. And this idea of a red&nbsp;
line and drawing a red line as a global community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:39.840
that machines must never decide who lives and who&nbsp;
dies is really a first step in that direction.

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:50.240
All right now we're going to cross the UN where&nbsp;
we're told the treaty has now been signed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:56.720
Generations before us thought that future&nbsp;
wars would be fought with toxic chemicals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.360 --> 00:23:04.080
and infectious germs. They thought our landscapes&nbsp;
would be littered with unexploded landlines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:09.680
and more recently it seemed inevitable&nbsp;
we were on a course to mass produce a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.680 --> 00:23:21.840
new class of merciless autonomous weapons&nbsp;
which use algorithms to decide who to kill.

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:36.080
We knew the dangers but we&nbsp;
thought we had no choice.

00:23:36.080 --> 00:23:42.880
Because we thought our enemies were too&nbsp;
inhuman to respect innocent lives. We thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:49.840
if we didn't, they would.

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:03.840
But each time we feared the worst,

00:24:07.120 --> 00:24:15.920
we were wrong. Together our nations have&nbsp;
ratified a new legally binding treaty. Together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:21.520
we prohibit weapons which select human&nbsp;
targets and attack without human control.

00:24:29.440 --> 00:24:35.840
Humanity must prevail on the&nbsp;
battlefield, and from today it will.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:50.800
So the third section of the film at its most&nbsp;
literal level is a fictionalized version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:57.200
the real world policy proposal for autonomous&nbsp;
weapons that is currently being debated. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.200 --> 00:25:04.000
that's at the heart of what the fictionalized&nbsp;
secretary general is saying in in this speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:10.240
is imagining what would our world look like on&nbsp;
the day that we actually agreed to a treaty on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:17.520
autonomous weapons. So in this last scene we see&nbsp;
the drone come in and is looming overhead and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:24.160
given all we've experienced up until this point&nbsp;
we assume that it's going to make the decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.160 --> 00:25:31.360
of who to kill of these two humans locked in this&nbsp;
complex standoff. But, it doesn't, it de-escalates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:38.880
the situation and both people make it out alive.&nbsp;
To me the drone represents our hopeful future with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:45.760
technology where it is used not to undermine our&nbsp;
humanity, but to build on it and to advance it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:52.800
In the earlier parts of the film, we see human&nbsp;
beings through the lens of technology as code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.800 --> 00:25:59.680
in screens, and through cameras but in this part&nbsp;
of the film we revert to a cinematic style of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:59.680 --> 00:26:06.080
filmmaking to really illuminate the human element&nbsp;
and to know that human beings are not just sensor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.080 --> 00:26:13.600
data, we're human beings. And in this scene we see&nbsp;
a tense standoff between two individuals who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:20.800
trying to navigate the complexity of one another's&nbsp;
humanity in impossible circumstances of war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:26.800
And what this scene really says to me and&nbsp;
what we see in the trade-offs and the dynamics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:33.040
between these two individuals is that there are&nbsp;
certain elements of being human and navigating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.040 --> 00:26:39.120
the humanity of another that will never be a&nbsp;
capability of an algorithm or of a machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:47.360
We start out with "if human: kill()" and we end at&nbsp;
"if human: protect()". The kill-protect dichotomy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.360 --> 00:26:54.160
is really representative of the options that lie&nbsp;
before us for our future with AI. There are very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.160 --> 00:27:01.600
few outcomes to be imagined where our future with&nbsp;
AI is incremental or middle of the road. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.600 --> 00:27:08.560
either going to be one which is flourishing and&nbsp;
incredible and advances our humanity or one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.560 --> 00:27:15.840
is very dark and destabilizing and undermines it.&nbsp;
And so this is the decision that's before us today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.560 --> 00:27:22.480
here with autonomous weapons is the first&nbsp;
step, which one are we going to choose to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:30.240
or to protect? If we can't even agree that we&nbsp;
don't want to let people legally build and sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:35.680
AI systems that can on their own go off and&nbsp;
decide who to kill and kill them, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:35.680 --> 00:27:39.760
who are we kidding that we're ever going to&nbsp;
draw the line anywhere, right? Whereas if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:39.760 --> 00:27:44.480
can win that battle, we've set the precedent&nbsp;
that there are some things that are off limits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:52.000
and we can steer this amazing technology towards&nbsp;
a future that I think we will all be really really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:58.240
excited to find ourselves in. So this film is&nbsp;
really timely in that while it's a work of fiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:03.040
it's something that is illuminating&nbsp;
a very very real world and applied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:10.080
circumstances that we're chatting through today.&nbsp;
It also is helping us to ground in what's ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:17.280
and as we think towards global action on this&nbsp;
issue we have this amazing opportunity, which is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:24.080
once in five-year meeting of the UN in December to&nbsp;
debate this issue specifically. And we have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:31.120
incredible moment in which we can draw this red&nbsp;
line and mitigate this risk of autonomous weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:37.200
and so that for us was key in developing this&nbsp;
film was we wanted to be able to remind people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:43.840
that this risk is here and but there's still&nbsp;
something we can do about it if we act now.

00:28:47.360 --> 00:28:51.840
So the policy prescription for autonomous&nbsp;
weapons, there is a great piece that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:56.960
been put forth by the ICRC, which is the&nbsp;
International Committee of the Red Cross.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:57.520 --> 00:29:04.720
It's an independent organization and they're&nbsp;
largely seen as the custodians of the law of war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.720 --> 00:29:10.240
And their policy proposal has three pillars.&nbsp;
The first being a prohibition, a legally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:10.240 --> 00:29:16.560
binding prohibition that's important on systems&nbsp;
with high degrees of unpredictable behavior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:22.640
The second, which is the policy proposal&nbsp;
that we explore in the film, is a legally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:28.720
binding prohibition on autonomous weapons&nbsp;
that are designed or used to target humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.280 --> 00:29:34.000
And the last one is the need to develop&nbsp;
regulations on other types of autonomous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:38.880
weapons. So you can think of that class as sort&nbsp;
of an autonomous fighter jet against autonomous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:44.080
fighter jet where the target is not actually a&nbsp;
human being. And these three pillars are to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.080 --> 00:29:49.520
debated by the UN at the meeting in December&nbsp;
as to whether or not to act and take these up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:49.520 --> 00:29:56.400
So a key element of a policy prescription for a&nbsp;
lethal autonomous weapons in this ICRC position is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.400 --> 00:30:03.840
the idea of a legally binding instrument, right.&nbsp;
Legally binding means new law, a new treaty. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:09.760
this is important because that does not mean&nbsp;
a political declaration or a political promise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:16.080
or some strong statement. There really is when we&nbsp;
look throughout the history of disarmament affairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:16.640 --> 00:30:24.160
no more important tool than a legally binding&nbsp;
instrument in order to exert a real meaningful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:30.400
effect on the behavior of states and also&nbsp;
generating a diplomatic and social stigma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.400 --> 00:30:34.880
around a class of weapons. We've seen this before&nbsp;
with land mines, we've seen it with bio weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:41.600
with chemical weapons. Legally binding is&nbsp;
central to whatever sort of path forward we take.

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:51.200
So there are plenty of historical examples&nbsp;
of where legally binding treaties were used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:51.200 --> 00:30:57.600
to mitigate the risks of powerful technologies,&nbsp;
the bioweapons ban, the chemical weapons ban,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:57.600 --> 00:31:03.600
bans on blinding lasers, the list goes on.&nbsp;
The bioweapons ban in particular is one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.600 --> 00:31:10.320
we really see as a great example for the future&nbsp;
with autonomous weapons. The bioweapons ban took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:16.720
the weaponization of biology and germ warfare off&nbsp;
of the table and that unlocked biology as a field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:23.520
to be one that we associate with new medicines,&nbsp;
curing diseases, and transforming agriculture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.520 --> 00:31:29.520
That's the fate that we hope a new legally binding&nbsp;
instrument will have for artificial intelligence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:36.400
By avoiding its weaponization, we will unlock its&nbsp;
positive potential to steer us towards a similarly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:36.400 --> 00:31:43.040
flourishing future for the science. The argument&nbsp;
that autonomous weapons have a high degree of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:50.800
military effectiveness and therefore no one's&nbsp;
going to agree to ban them probably isn't true.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:51.840 --> 00:32:00.240
Chemical weapons were quite effective in World War&nbsp;
I. If you read the memoir of the Russian gentleman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:09.360
who was in charge of a large part of their&nbsp;
biological weapons program in the 60s, 70s, 80s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:15.200
he believes that Russian use of&nbsp;
biological weapons in World War II was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:23.840
extremely important in helping to defeat the&nbsp;
Germans. And so there's no doubt that the US and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.840 --> 00:32:30.560
Russia believed biological weapons were a very&nbsp;
very important part of their defense programs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:31.760 --> 00:32:36.640
And yet, we were able to ban them.&nbsp;
The argument that was used initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:45.840
in the US by biologists and chemists was that&nbsp;
if these weapons become widely available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:53.120
they will be cheap effective weapons of mass&nbsp;
destruction that will proliferate to non-state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:53.120 --> 00:32:58.240
actors, to rogue nations, to smaller&nbsp;
countries, and tip the balance of power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:06.320
away from the major powers and thereby&nbsp;
reducing the security of the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.680 --> 00:33:15.120
That argument was persuasive to Richard Nixon who&nbsp;
unilaterally abandoned the US biological weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:21.600
program not because it was ineffective but because&nbsp;
it would become effective and if that happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:21.600 --> 00:33:25.280
would reduce the security of the&nbsp;
United States. So are we doomed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:31.120
right? Is our fate sealed with autonomous weapons&nbsp;
and we should all just invest in a bunker? Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:36.800
there's many reasons why you may sincerely want to&nbsp;
invest in a bunker, but I sincerely believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.800 --> 00:33:41.840
autonomous weapons should not be one of them.&nbsp;
And so with this film we really aim to show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:51.840
that yes action is possible, this is what it could&nbsp;
look like, and this is why we need to do it now.

00:33:53.840 --> 00:34:00.720
So what is it that you can do about autonomous&nbsp;
weapons? Learn more about it. You can find out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:00.720 --> 00:34:06.560
more by going to autonomousweapons.org. If you&nbsp;
want a future free of slaughterbots please share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:06.560 --> 00:34:12.000
this film on social media and please make sure&nbsp;
that the people who are going to be making these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:17.840
decisions actually understand how much you care&nbsp;
about this because this is an incredibly important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:18.720 --> 00:34:28.320
watershed. And try to move your country your&nbsp;
fellow citizens in the direction of supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:34.400
a treaty. If you are a journalist, you have&nbsp;
an enormous opportunity to actually influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:34.400 --> 00:34:40.720
what happens to get the happy ending we saw in&nbsp;
this film. The main reason that this hasn't been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:40.720 --> 00:34:45.920
solved already is because these perspectives are&nbsp;
almost never made available to the broader public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:51.920
and therefore the decision makers never hear&nbsp;
from the broader public about the need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:57.360
actually do something about it. If you are a&nbsp;
person who works in artificial intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:57.360 --> 00:35:02.480
you should know that the vast majority of your&nbsp;
colleagues already support this idea of banning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:07.760
lethal autonomous weapons. We've done a lot of&nbsp;
work at FLI to develop both open letters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:07.760 --> 00:35:13.760
pledges to never develop or work on these systems&nbsp;
and to support the kind of regulation that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:13.760 --> 00:35:20.320
being put forth by the ICRC. So please make your&nbsp;
voice heard and don't let a few rotten eggs you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:20.320 --> 00:35:25.600
know ruin the reputation of your field for you.&nbsp;
The same way that biologists stood up and said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.600 --> 00:35:31.920
hey we want biology to stand for saving lives,&nbsp;
make sure that your field gets remembered as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:41.840
a field that helps find new solutions, not the&nbsp;
field that kind of ruined our open society with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:41.840 --> 00:35:47.600
slaughterbots. And if you're a member of the&nbsp;
diplomatic community it's time to act it's time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.600 --> 00:35:54.720
to adopt these types of policy frameworks that&nbsp;
are clear, that are concise, and that are ready to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.720 --> 00:36:01.200
go. And so encouraging bold action from our policy&nbsp;
makers is really what the world needs today. So at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:09.760
present the topic of autonomous weapons is being&nbsp;
discussed at the CCW in Geneva. The CCW process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:10.320 --> 00:36:17.360
is a consensus process so it requires essentially&nbsp;
every nation to go along with an agreement to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:17.360 --> 00:36:24.240
have a discussion, to go along with drafting the&nbsp;
terms of a treaty, and to agree to the treaty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:33.040
An alternative is the UN general assembly which&nbsp;
doesn't require unanimity and I think that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.040 --> 00:36:40.320
reflect better the interests of the people&nbsp;
of the world who over and over again in polls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:46.400
have shown that they are very strongly opposed&nbsp;
to the development of these kinds of weapons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:54.720
If you are part of the United Nations CCW&nbsp;
process then please vote "no" in December&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:54.720 --> 00:37:01.760
to any extension of the CCW's mandate beyond&nbsp;
this year unless there's a clear resolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:08.240
to ban lethal autonomous weapons. Otherwise,&nbsp;
the CCW is not part of the solution it's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.240 --> 00:37:13.040
standing in the way of the solution preventing&nbsp;
this issue from going to a different forum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.320 --> 00:37:20.160
that can actually handle it and give us the&nbsp;
ban that we need. Steering the future of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:35.840
technology is hard but it's worth doing because&nbsp;
the upside if we get it right is just tremendous.

